INT. FUNICULAR TICKET BOOTH - MORNING

A glass booth no bigger than a closet, perched at the bottom of a steep hill railway. Brass fittings, a gentle hum of cable overhead.

ELOISE VANTERPOOL (30s, soft-spoken, wearing a belted coat) stamps tickets one by one. Across the counter, DESMOND "DEZ" OKAFOR (30s, rumpled tweed, a paper bag of pastries) hovers.

DEZ
Eloise, you have stamped that same ticket three times.

ELOISE
Then it's very well stamped, Dez.

He slides the bag through the slot. She peeks inside and smiles.

DEZ
Apricot. Your favorite.

ELOISE
You remembered.

DEZ
I remember everything about you. Except where I left my umbrella.

The booth door opens. PRUDENCE LAWSON (60s, silver bun, carrying a knitted bag) steps in, shaking the damp from her shoulders.

PRUDENCE
Two for the summit, please. And don't let me interrupt.

ELOISE
You aren't, Mrs. Lawson.

DEZ ^
You aren't, Mrs. Lawson.

PRUDENCE
Dear me. You two even speak alike.

A tall man, THADDEUS BRIGHT (40s, conductor's cap), raps on the glass from outside.

THADDEUS
Car's leaving, Prudence! Mind the step!

Prudence waves, tucks her tickets away, and exits. Eloise and Dez watch the car rise.
